\f2\b \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 WAAS
\f1\b0  is calculated from the 
\f2\b absolute values of every significant IPCA (interaction-principal-component) score of a genotype or environment
\f1\b0 . Hence WAAS is 
\f2\b not
\f1\b0  adding \'93all other IPCAs\'94 separately; it combines 
\f2\b every retained IPCA\'92s absolute score, each scaled by how much GE interaction that axis explains
\f1\b0 .\
A small WAAS means the genotype\'92s (or environment\'92s) absolute IPCA scores are small on all axes after weighting\'97i.e. it interacts weakly and is 
\f2\b stable across sites/years
\f1\b0 .\
A large WAAS means large absolute IPCA scores on one or more heavily weighted axes\'97i.e. strong, unstable GE interaction.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec3 Olivoto, T., L\'facio, A.D., Silva, J.A.G., Marchioro, V.S., Souza, V.Q., & Jost, E. (2019). 
\f3\i Mean performance and stability in multi\uc0\u8208 environment trials II: Selection based on multiple traits
\f2\i0 . Agronomy Journal, 111(6), 2961\'962970.
\f1\b0 \
That paper introduces WAAS as a way to synthesise all significant IPCAs from an AMMI analysis into a single stability metric by taking the weighted average of the absolute scores, with weights proportional to each IPCA\'92s share of interaction sum of squares.\
\
